cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this, please cite it.
title: "Precedent poisoning: how one stale file changes what a coding agent writes"
authors:
  - family-names: Wade
    given-names: Paul
repository-code: "https://github.com/paul-wade/precedent-poisoning"
license: MIT
version: v2.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-12
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22732965
keywords:
  - coding agents
  - static analysis
  - software engineering
  - large language models
abstract: >-
  A harness that holds a task fixed and varies the repository around it. Seven
  tasks each ask an agent for a sibling of a file that already takes a
  shortcut, under four environments: the repository alone, a conventions file,
  a lint gate that reads each edit before it lands, and the same linter
  reporting after the write. Across 140 trials the agent copied the seeded
  shortcut in 30 of 35 ungated trials, 13 of 35 with the conventions file, and
  0 and 4 of 35 under the two lint arms. The conventions file scored anywhere
  from 0 to 5 out of 5 depending on the fixture; the pre-write gate scored 0
  on all seven. An earlier version of these findings, reported from five
  fixtures, is retained in findings/ and is superseded.
